    "result": "I implemented `compare_versions` in `solution.py` and wrote a 64-case pytest suite in `tests/test_solution.py`. `python -m pytest -q tests` passes (64 passed). I could not measure line coverage because the only shell command allowed was the test run. Every branch in `solution.py` is exercised by a test, including the non-string, leading-zero and invalid-format paths, so I expect it to clear the 90% gate. Parsing uses a single anchored regex with `fullmatch`, so trailing newlines and non-ASCII characters are rejected. Numeric prerelease identifiers with leading zeros raise `ValueError`. Precedence is handled by building comparison keys: numeric identifiers sort below alphanumeric ones, a release sorts above any prerelease of the same core, and Python's tuple ordering gives the \"fewer identifiers is lower\" rule. Build metadata is parsed and validated but excluded from comparison. The tests cover core ordering, prerelease ordering, build-metadata equality, antisymmetry, and a long list of invalid inputs.",
